y="88.2" r="1.6" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><circle cx="325.8" cy="165.8" r="4.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="325.8" cy="165.8" r="1.6" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><circle cx="325.8" cy="204.6" r="4.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="325.8" cy="204.6" r="1.6" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><polyline points="70,190 240,190 240,62.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="90" y1="206" x2="143.7" y2="206" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="150,206 141.8,209.8 141.8,202.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="156" y="206" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">5.0 A</text><text x="155" y="226" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">PQ = 0.20 m</text><text x="252" y="126.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">QR = 0.15 m</text><circle cx="70" cy="190" r="3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="60" y="180" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">P</text><circle cx="240" cy="190" r="3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="180" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Q</text><circle cx="240" cy="62.5" r="3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="52.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">R</text><line x1="290" y1="240" x2="325.1" y2="240" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="330,240 323.7,243 323.7,237" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="336" y="240" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">x</text><line x1="290" y1="240" x2="290" y2="204.9" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="290,200 293,206.3 287,206.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="290" y="192" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">y</text><text x="344" y="14" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">B = 0.40 T along +z</text></svg>', 'A wire PQR: PQ = 0.20 m along +x and QR = 0.15 m along +y, carrying 5.0 A from P to R, in a uniform 0.40 T field along +z (out of the page).'),
('66b9e387-7067-5681-948b-c7e8eeae9780', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 44, 44, 'A 0.50 m wire lies along the x-axis carrying 2.0 A. The field has a component of 0.30 T along x and 0.40 T along y. (a) Explain which component produces a force. (b) Calculate the force on the wire. (c) Calculate the angle between the wire and the resultant field. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.4', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Only the y-component, which is at right angles to the wire. (b) F = 0.40 × 2.0 × 0.50 = 0.40 N, along z. (c) tan θ = 0.40 ÷ 0.30, so θ = tan⁻¹ 1.333 = 53.1°; check: B sin θ = 0.50 × sin 53.1° = 0.40 T. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: perpendicular component only. C1: F = B⊥IL. C1: angle. B1: consistent with B sin θ. A1: F = 0.40 N.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgb5b03794-t fgb5b03794-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgb5b03794-t">Field components</title><desc id="fgb5b03794-d">The 0.50 m wire lies along the x-axis carrying 2.0 A. The field has a component of 0.30 T along x and 0.40 T along y, drawn from the same point.</desc><line x1="70" y1="180" x2="153.7" y2="180" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="160,180 151.8,183.8 151.8,176.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="174" y="180" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">0.30 T along x</text><line x1="70" y1="180" x2="70" y2="66.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="70,60 73.8,68.2 66.2,68.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="70" y="46" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">0.40 T along y</text><circle cx="70" cy="180" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg>', 'The 0.50 m wire lies along the x-axis carrying 2.0 A. The field has a component of 0.30 T along x and 0.40 T along y, drawn from the same point.'),
('2cb27a03-14e3-5e6b-8d1e-691a17a04471', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 45, 45, 'A rectangular coil of 40 turns, 0.050 m by 0.040 m, carries 0.50 A in a uniform field of 0.20 T. The torque on it is NBIA cos φ, where φ is the angle between the plane of the coil and the field. (a) Calculate NBIA. (b) Calculate the torque when φ = 60°. (c) Explain why the torque is zero when φ = 90°. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.004', 'N m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) NBIA = 40 × 0.20 × 0.50 × 0.050 × 0.040 = 8.0 × 10⁻³ N m. (b) Torque = 8.0 × 10⁻³ × cos 60° = 0.00400 N m. (c) With the plane perpendicular to B, the forces on opposite sides act along the same line, so they have no turning effect. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: area in m². C1: NBIA. M1: × cos φ. C1: value. B1: forces in line. A1: torque = 0.0040 N m.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('026034ac-8a3e-5023-8672-6e335fb4d877', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 46, 46, 'The Earth''s field at a place is 50 µT at a dip angle of 65°, in the north–vertical plane. A horizontal cable 20 m long carries 100 A. (a) Calculate the force on the cable when it runs east–west. (b) Calculate the force when it runs north–south. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.09063', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) East–west is at right angles to the whole field: F = 50 × 10⁻⁶ × 100 × 20 = 0.10 N. (b) North–south: only the vertical component, 50 × sin 65° = 45.3 µT, is perpendicular: F = 45.32 × 10⁻⁶ × 100 × 20 = 0.0906 N. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: east–west uses full B. M1: north–south uses vertical component. C1: B sin 65°. C1: force. A1: F = 0.0906 N.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg52afc1ed-t fg52afc1ed-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg52afc1ed-t">Angle of dip</title><desc id="fg52afc1ed-d">The Earth''s field of 50 µT in the north–vertical plane, pointing downwards at a dip angle of 65° below the horizontal.</desc><line x1="50" y1="50" x2="320" y2="50" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:4 3"/><text x="320" y="38" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">north (horizontal)</text><line x1="50" y1="50" x2="123" y2="50" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="129.3,50 121.1,53.8 121.1,46.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="129.3" y1="50" x2="129.3" y2="213.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 3"/><polygon points="129.3,220 125.5,211.8 133.1,211.8" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="50" y1="50" x2="126.6" y2="214.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="129.3,220 122.4,214.2 129.3,211" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="137.3" y="135" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">vertical component</text><text x="75.6" y="141" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">50 µT</text><path d="M94,50 A44,44 0 0,1 68.6,89.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="98.9" y="81.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">65°</text></svg>', 'The Earth''s field of 50 µT in the north–vertical plane, pointing downwards at a dip angle of 65° below the horizontal.'),
('2f2b0f18-7177-5be3-bd37-d25a489cc132', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 47, 47, 'A metal rod of mass 0.050 kg rests across two smooth rails 0.20 m apart, which slope at 30° to the horizontal. A uniform vertical field of 0.50 T acts, and a current flows through the rod. (a) Explain why the magnetic force on the rod is horizontal. (b) Show that, for equilibrium, the magnetic force F satisfies F = mg tan 30°. (c) Calculate the current needed. Give only the answer to (c). Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.832', 'A', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The force is perpendicular to both the horizontal rod and the vertical field, so it is horizontal. (b) Resolving along the slope: F cos 30° = mg sin 30°, so F = mg tan 30°. (c) F = 0.050 × 9.81 × tan 30° = 0.283 N; I = F/(BL) = 0.2832 ÷ (0.50 × 0.20) = 2.83 A. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: horizontal force. M1: resolve along the slope. C1: F = mg tan 30°. C1: I = F/(BL). C1: value. A1: I = 2.83 A.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('029b9c13-1cd4-56eb-b425-065c3f062fa0', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 48, 48, 'A wire feels 0.36 N when at right angles to a uniform field. (a) Write an expression for the force at angle θ. (b) Calculate the smallest angle between 0° and 90° at which the force is 0.18 N. (c) State the range of angles between 0° and 180° for which the force exceeds 0.18 N. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '30', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) F = 0.36 sin θ. (b) sin θ = 0.18 ÷ 0.36 = 0.50, so θ = sin⁻¹ 0.50 = 30°. (c) Between 30° and 150°. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: F = F_max sin θ. C1: sin θ = 0.50. C1: 30°. B1: 30° < θ < 150°. A1: θ = 30°.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('f0f3faa3-af31-5e9b-84da-54a44a4bbcee', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 49, 49, '(a) By resolving the field into components along and at right angles to a wire, show that the force on the wire is BIL sin θ. (b) Calculate the force on a 0.40 m wire carrying 6.0 A at 35° to a field of 0.15 T. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.2065', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The component B cos θ along the wire exerts no force; the component B sin θ is perpendicular, giving F = (B sin θ)IL. (b) F = 0.15 × 6.0 × 0.40 × sin 35° = 0.206 N. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: parallel component gives no force. B1: perpendicular component B sin θ. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: F = 0.206 N.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('2ccf207a-a39d-594d-a087-ae857c08089c', '451c0a56-584e-41c1-958b-7b940530308c', '1f9b9b3e-2978-4618-bfd5-4659023c5bdb', 'a-level', '24.5', '9702 coursebook 24.5 · Currents crossing fields', 50, 50, 'Two parallel rails are 0.10 m apart in a vertical field of 0.40 T. A straight rod is laid across them at 60° to the rails, and carries 3.0 A. (a) Calculate the length of rod between the rails. (b) Calculate the magnetic force on the rod. (c) Calculate the component of this force along the rails. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.12', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L = 0.10 ÷ sin 60° = 0.115 m. (b) The rod is perpendicular to the vertical field: F = 0.40 × 3.0 × 0.1155 = 0.139 N, perpendicular to the rod. (c) Along the rails: 0.1386 × sin 60° = 0.12 N, the same as for a rod at right angles to the rails. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: L = d/sin 60°. C1: F = BIL. M1: resolve along rails. C1: value. B1: equals BId. A1: F = 0.12 N.', 6, NULL, NULL)
